Interesting & cool.  I wasn't expecting it to be that straightforward.

I'll have to go and look at the relative complexity of tying that text
to different events.  Ultimately I would like to be able to have
something along the lines of the potential for a readable "library" of
sorts - not spellbooks, just books/scrolls/etc.  There's a few reasons
I think that could be interesting in-game - for one, I'm trying to look
at ways for players to communicate with one another via the bones files
(leaving a message - scribing a notebook, keeping in sack), though
that's not my primary objective.  I also think it could potentially
enhance the storytelling aspect of the game.

Obviously having player-writable texts is a differently-colored horse.
I expect that would require a new mechanism on top of com_pager() that
would direct the game to an external source for the text itself.  So
that is very much secondary to what I'm actually looking at doing,
which would use only static text.

Essentially I'm looking for the most efficient ways to create the kind
of thing I was talking about in my earlier thread re: baby nethack.  I
determined that level creation is reasonably simple, even without the
lev_comp app, and so I'm looking at building a short and entirely
optional branch off dlvl1 that would supply a newbie player with some
useful stuff and a lot of informative (not spoily, however) text and
opportunities to experiment in a somewhat guided fashion.  I'm not sure
yet how much of what I'm aiming to do I'll actually be ABLE to get
done, but at this point it certainly sounds like the difficulty level
is turning out to be far lower than I had originally anticipated.

